"hadiths[73]{hadithnumber,text}:"
544,"Abd al-Wahhab ibn Abd al-Hakam al-Warraq al-Baghdadi narrated to us, that Yahya ibn Sulaym narrated to us, from Ubayd Allah, from Nafi', from Ibn 'Umar, who said: ""I travelled with the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him), Abu Bakr, 'Umar, and 'Uthman, and they used to pray Dhuhr and 'Asr as two rak'ats each, and they did not pray any voluntary prayers before or after them."" 'Abdullah (ibn 'Umar) said: ""Had I been praying voluntary prayers before or after them, I would have completed them (as four rak'ats)."" He (the narrator) said: ""And in this chapter (there are narrations) from 'Umar, 'Ali, Ibn 'Abbas, Anas, 'Imran ibn Husayn, and 'A'ishah."" Abu 'Isa (al-Tirmidhi) said: ""The hadith of Ibn 'Umar is a hasan gharib hadith; we do not know it except from the hadith of Yahya ibn Sulaym in this form."" Muhammad ibn Isma'il (al-Bukhari) said: ""And this hadith has been narrated from Ubayd Allah ibn 'Umar, from a man from the family of Suraqah, from 'Abdullah ibn 'Umar."" Abu 'Isa said: ""And it has been narrated from 'Atiyyah al-'Awfi, from Ibn 'Umar, that the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) used to perform voluntary prayers while travelling before and after the obligatory prayer."" And it has been authentically established from the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) that he used to shorten (the prayer) while travelling, as did Abu Bakr, 'Umar, and 'Uthman during the early part of his caliphate. And the practice (al-'amal) according to this is held by the majority of the people of knowledge among the Companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) and others. And it has been narrated from 'A'ishah that she used to complete the prayer while travelling. And the practice is upon what has been narrated from the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) and his Companions, and this is the position of al-Shafi'i, Ahmad, and Ishaq, except that al-Shafi'i says: ""Shortening is a concession (rukhsah) for him during travel; if he completes the prayer, it suffices him."""
545,"Abu An-Nadrah said:""Imran bin Husain was asked about the traveler's prayer. He said: 'I performed Hajj with the Messenger of Allah"
546,"Anas bin Malik narrated:""We prayed Zuhr as four with the Prophet in Al-Madinah"
547,"Ibn Abbas narrated:""The Prophet went from Al-Madinah to Makkah"
548,"Yahya bin Abi Ishaq Al-Hadrami narrated :that Anas bin Malik said: ""We went with the Messenger of Allah from Al-Madinah to Makkah"
549,"Ibn Abbas narrated:""The Messenger of Allah traveled on a journey and he prayed two Rak'ah for nineteen days."" Ibn Abbas said: ""So when we would stay somewhere for nineteen (days) we would pray two Rak'ah"
550,"Al-Bara bin Azib said:""I accompanied the Messenger of Allah on eighteen journeys"
551,"Ibn Umar narrated:""I prayed Zuhr with the Prophet on a journey as two Rak'ah"
552,"# Translation of the Hadith

**Muḥammad ibn ʿUbaid al-Muḥārībī al-Kūfī narrated to us, saying:** ʿAlī ibn Hāshim narrated to us, from Ibn Abī Laylā, from ʿAṭiyyah and Nāfiʿ, from Ibn ʿUmar (may Allāh be pleased with both of them), who said:

""I prayed with the Prophet (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa-sallam) both while resident and while travelling. I prayed with him while resident: Ẓuhr as four rakʿāt, followed by two rakʿāt; and I prayed with him while travelling: Ẓuhr as two rakʿāt, followed by two rakʿāt; and ʿAṣr as two rakʿāt, and he did not pray anything after it; and Maghrib was the same whether resident or travelling — three rakʿāt, neither reduced while resident nor while travelling, for it is the *witr* (odd-numbered prayer) of the daytime — and after it, two rakʿāt.""

Abū ʿĪsā (al-Tirmidhī) said: **""This is a ḥasan (good) hadith.""**

He further said: **""I heard Muḥammad (i.e., al-Bukhārī) say: 'Ibn Abī Laylā has not narrated any hadith more pleasing to me than this, yet I do not narrate anything from him.'""**

---

*Note: This hadith is recorded in Sunan al-Tirmidhī and illustrates the Prophetic practice (sunnah) of the voluntary (nafl) prayers performed alongside the obligatory (farḍ) prayers, both during residence and travel.*"
553,"Mu'adh bin Jabal narrated:""While the Prophet as at the Battle of Tabuk"
554,"Abd al-Samad ibn Sulayman narrated to us, Zakariyya al-Lu'lu'i narrated to us, Abu Bakr al-A'yan narrated to us, 'Ali ibn al-Madini narrated to us, Ahmad ibn Hanbal narrated to us, and Qutaybah narrated this Hadith, meaning the Hadith of Mu'adh (may Allah be pleased with him). The Hadith of Mu'adh is a Hasan Gharib Hadith; Qutaybah is the sole narrator of it, and we do not know of anyone other than him who narrated it from al-Layth. The Hadith of al-Layth from Yazid ibn Abi Habib from Abu al-Tufayl from Mu'adh is a Gharib Hadith. What is well-known among the scholars is the Hadith of Mu'adh as narrated by Abu al-Zubayr from Abu al-Tufayl from Mu'adh (may Allah be pleased with him) that the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) combined, during the expedition of Tabuk, between Dhuhr and 'Asr, and between Maghrib and 'Isha'. This was narrated by Qurrah ibn Khalid, Sufyan al-Thawri, Malik, and others from Abu al-Zubayr al-Makki. It is with reference to this Hadith that Imam al-Shafi'i (may Allah have mercy on him) issued his ruling. Ahmad and Ishaq (may Allah have mercy on them both) stated: ""There is no harm in combining the two prayers during travel in the time of either one of them."""
555,"Nafi narrated:""Ibn Umar had been requested to urgently attend to one of his wives"
556,"Abbad bin Tamim narrated from his uncle:""The Messenger of Allah went out with the people to seek rain. So he led them in prayer for two Rak'ah"
557,"Qutaybah narrated to us, who said: Layth ibn Sa‘d narrated to us, from Khalid ibn Yazid, from Sa‘id ibn Abi Hilal, from Yazid ibn ‘Abdullah, from ‘Umays, the freedman of Abu al-Laham, from Abu al-Laham, that he saw the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) at the stones of oil, praying for rain, raising his palms in supplication. Abu ‘Isa said: Qutaybah said this in the hadith from Abu al-Laham, and we do not know of any other hadith from the Prophet (peace be upon him) from him except this one. ‘Umays, the freedman of Abu al-Laham, narrated other hadiths from the Prophet (peace be upon him) and was a Companion."
558,"It is narrated from Hisham bin Ishaq - and he was from Ibn Abdullah bin Kinanah - from his father who said:""Al-Walid bin Uqbah"
559,"(Another chain) from Hisham bin Ishaq bin Abdullah bin Kinanah"
560,"Narrated Ibn 'Abbas: that the Prophet (ﷺ) prayed during an eclipse, so he recited, then bowed, then recited, then bowed, then recited, then bowed - three times - then he prostrated with two prostrations, and the other (unit) was similar to it.

[Abu 'Eisa said:] There are narrations on this topic from 'Ali, 'Aishah, 'Abdullah bin 'Amr, An-Nu'man bin Bashir, Al-Mughirah bin Shu'bah, Abu Mas'ud, Abu Bakrah, Samurah, Abu Musa Al-Ashari, Ibn Mas'ud, Asma' bint Abu Bakr As-Siddiq, Ibn 'Umar, Qabisah Al-Hilali, Jabir bin 'Abdullah, 'Abdur-Rahman bin Samurah, and Ubayy bin Ka'b.

Abu 'Eisa said: The Hadith of Ibn 'Abbas is a Hasan Sahih Hadith. And it has been narrated from Ibn 'Abbas, from the Prophet (ﷺ) that he prayed during an eclipse with four Rak'ah and four prostrations. This is what Ash-Shafi'i, Ahmad, and Ishaq say.

He said: The people of knowledge differed regarding the recitation in the eclipse prayer. Some of the people of knowledge held the view that the recitation in it during the day should be silent. Others held the view that the recitation in it should be aloud, similar to the two 'Eid prayers and the Friday prayer, and this is what Malik, Ahmad, and Ishaq say - they hold the view that it should be recited aloud. Ash-Shafi'i said: It should not be recited aloud in it.

Both of these two reports have been confirmed as authentic from the Prophet (ﷺ). It has been confirmed from him that he prayed four Rak'ah with four prostrations, and it has also been confirmed from him that he prayed six Rak'ah with four prostrations. This, according to the people of knowledge, is permissible depending on the length of the eclipse: if the eclipse is prolonged, then he prays six Rak'ah with four prostrations, and that is permissible; and if he prays four Rak'ah with four prostrations and lengthens the recitation, that is also permissible. Our companions hold the view that the eclipse prayer should be performed in congregation, for both a solar and a lunar eclipse."
561,"Muhammad bin 'Abdul-Malik bin Abi Ash-Shawarib narrated to us, Yazid bin Zurai' narrated to us, Ma'mar narrated to us, from Az-Zuhri, from 'Urwah, from 'Aishah, that she said: ""The sun eclipsed during the time of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), so the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) led the people in prayer, and he prolonged the recitation, then he performed a prostration (bowing), and he prolonged the bowing, then he raised his head and prolonged the recitation, which was shorter than the first, then he bowed and prolonged the bowing, which was shorter than the first, then he raised his head and prostrated. Then he did the same in the second unit (Rak'ah).""

Abu 'Eisa said: ""This Hadith is Hasan Sahih. This is what Ash-Shafi'i, Ahmad, and Ishaq say; they view the eclipse prayer as four Rak'ahs with four prostrations.""

Ash-Shafi'i said: ""He recites in the first Rak'ah the Opening of the Book (Al-Fatihah) and something similar to Surat Al-Baqarah, silently if it is during the day. Then he performs a prolonged bowing similar in length to his recitation. Then he raises his head with the Takbir, and remains standing as he was, and recites again the Opening of the Book and something similar to Surat Al 'Imran. Then he performs a prolonged bowing similar to his recitation. Then he raises his head and says: 'Allah hears the one who praises Him (Sami' Allahu Liman Hamidah).' Then he performs two complete prostrations, remaining in each prostration for a duration similar to what he remained in his bowing. Then he stands and recites the Opening of the Book and something similar to Surat An-Nisa. Then he performs a prolonged bowing similar to his recitation. Then he raises his head with the Takbir and remains standing. Then he recites something similar to Surat Al-Ma'idah. Then he performs a prolonged bowing similar to his recitation. Then he raises and says: 'Allah hears the one who praises Him.' Then he performs two prostrations, then performs the Tashahhud and gives the Salam."""
562,"Samurah bin Jundah narrated:""The Prophet led us in prayer during an eclipse; we did not hear his voice"
563,"Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn Abān narrated to us, Ibrāhīm ibn Ṣadaqah narrated to us, from Sufyān ibn Ḥusayn, from al-Zuhrī, from ʿUrwah, from ʿĀʾishah (may Allah be pleased with her), that the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) performed the eclipse prayer and recited aloud therein.

Abū ʿĪsā said: This is a ḥasan ḥadīth. Abū Isḥāq al-Fazārī narrated a similar version from Sufyān ibn Ḥusayn. This hadith is the basis for the position of Mālik ibn Anas, Aḥmad, and Isḥāq."
564,"Narrated Salim: from his father, that the Prophet (ﷺ) led one of the two groups in prayer for one Rak'ah while the other group faced the enemy. Then they withdrew and took the position of the others, and the others came and he led them in another Rak'ah, then he said the Salam for them. Then these stood and completed their Rak'ah, and those stood and completed their Rak'ah.

Abu Eisa said: This Hadith is Hasan Sahih. Musa bin 'Uqbah has also narrated similarly from Nafi' from Ibn 'Umar.

He said: There are narrations on this topic from Jabir, Hudhaifah, Zaid bin Thabit, Ibn 'Abbas, Abu Hurairah, Ibn Mas'ud, Sahl bin Abu Hathmah, Abu 'Ayyash Az-Zuraqi whose name is Zaid bin Samit, and Abu Bakrah.

Abu Eisa said: Malik bin Anas held the view regarding Salat Al-Khawf according to the Hadith of Sahl bin Abu Hathmah, and this is the view of Ash-Shafi'i. Ahmad said: ""The Salat Al-Khawf has been narrated from the Prophet (ﷺ) in various forms, and I do not know of anything in this topic except an authentic Hadith, and I prefer the Hadith of Sahl bin Abu Hathmah."" Similarly, Ishaq bin Ibrahim said: ""The narrations from the Prophet (ﷺ) about Salat Al-Khawf are established."" He held the view that whatever has been narrated from the Prophet (ﷺ) regarding Salat Al-Khawf is permissible, and this depends on the degree of fear. Ishaq said: ""We do not prefer the Hadith of Sahl bin Abu Hathmah over the other narrations."""
565,"Sahl bin Abi Hathmah said about Salat Al-Khawf:""The Imam stands facing the Qiblah while a group of them stand with him"
566,"(Abu Eisa said:) Muhammad bin Bash-har said: ""I asked Yahya bin Sa'eed (narrators in no. 565) about this Hadith. So he narrated it to me from Shu'bah"
567,"# Scholarly Translation of the Hadith

It was narrated by Mālik ibn Anas, from Yazīd ibn Rūmān, from Ṣāliḥ ibn Khawwāt, from one who performed the Prayer of Fear (*Ṣalāt al-Khawf*) with the Prophet ﷺ, and he mentioned something similar to the preceding narration.

Abū ʿĪsā (al-Tirmidhī) said: **""This is a ḥasan ṣaḥīḥ hadith.""**

He further stated: **""Upon this position stand Mālik, al-Shāfiʿī, Aḥmad, and Isḥāq.""**

It was also narrated from more than one source that the Prophet ﷺ prayed with one of the two groups one rakʿah at a time, such that the Prophet ﷺ completed two rakʿahs while each group completed one rakʿah each.

Abū ʿĪsā (al-Tirmidhī) said: **""Abū ʿAyyāsh al-Zuraqī — his name is Zayd ibn Ṣāmit.""**

---

*Note: This narration pertains to the description of Ṣalāt al-Khawf (the Prayer of Fear), a specialized prayer performed during times of conflict or danger, and reflects the jurisprudential positions of the four major Imams regarding its method of performance.*"
568,"Abu Ad-Darda narrated:""I performed eleven prostrations (of recitation) with the Messenger of Allah"
569,"(Another chain in which) Abu Ad-Darda narrated:""I performed eleven prostrations with the Messenger of Allah"
570,"# Translation of the Hadith

**Narrated by Nusayr ibn 'Alī, who narrated from 'Īsā ibn Yūnus, from al-A'mash, from Mujāhid, who said:**

""We were in the presence of Ibn 'Umar (may Allah be pleased with both of them) when he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said: **'Permit the women to go to the mosques at night.'**

Thereupon, his son (may Allah have mercy on him) said: 'By Allah, we shall not permit them, lest they take it as a means of deception (and mischief).'

Ibn 'Umar (may Allah be pleased with both of them) then said: 'May Allah do such-and-such to you! I say that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said (this), and you say, ""We shall not permit them""?!'""

He (the narrator) said: ""In this chapter (there are also narrations) from Abū Hurayrah, Zaynab the wife of 'Abdullāh ibn Mas'ūd, and Zayd ibn Khālid (may Allah be pleased with them all).""

Abū 'Īsā (al-Tirmidhī) said: ""The Hadith of Ibn 'Umar is a **Hasan Sahīh** (good and authentic) Hadith.""

---

*This Hadith is recorded in Jāmi' al-Tirmidhī and is also found in the collections of al-Bukhārī and Muslim, among others. It establishes the permissibility of women attending the mosques, particularly during the night, while also illustrating the concern of the Companions regarding the preservation of proper conduct.*"
571,"Tariq bin Abdullah Al-Muharibi narrated that :the Messenger of Allah said: ""When you are in Salat then do not spit on your right"
572,"Anas bin Malik narrated that :the Messenger of Allah said: ""To spit in the Masjid is a sin"
573,"Abu Hurairah narrated:""We prostrated with the Messenger of Allah in: 'Read! In the Name of your Lord' and 'When the heaven is split asunder"
574,"(Another chain in which) Abu Hurairah narrated :from the Prophet similarly"
575,"Ibn Abbas narrated:""The Messenger of Allah prostrated for it - meaning (in Surat) An-Najm - and so did the Muslims"
576,"Yahya bin Musa narrated to us, Waki' narrated to us, from Ibn Abi Dhi'b, from Yazid bin 'Abdullah bin Qusait, from 'Ata' bin Yasar, from Zaid bin Thabit who said: ""I recited An-Najm (Surat An-Najm) to the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) and he did not prostrate during it.""

Abu 'Eisa said: The Hadith of Zaid bin Thabit is a Hasan Sahih Hadith. Some of the people of knowledge have interpreted this Hadith, saying: ""The Prophet (ﷺ) only refrained from prostrating because when Zaid bin Thabit recited it and did not prostrate, the Prophet (ﷺ) did not prostrate."" They said: ""The prostration is obligatory upon whoever hears it,"" and they did not permit leaving it. They said: ""If a man hears it while he is not in a state of purity, then when he performs ablution he should prostrate."" This is the view of Sufyan Ath-Thawri and the people of Kufa, and it is what Ishaq says.

Some of the people of knowledge said: ""The prostration is only required for one who intends to prostrate during it and seeks its merit,"" and they permitted leaving it if that was one's intention. They cited as evidence the Marfu' Hadith of Zaid bin Thabit, where he said: ""I recited An-Najm to the Prophet (ﷺ) and he did not prostrate during it."" They said: ""If the prostration were obligatory, the Prophet (ﷺ) would not have left Zaid without correcting him, such that Zaid would prostrate and the Prophet (ﷺ) would prostrate.""

They also cited as evidence the Hadith of 'Umar, that he recited a verse containing a prostration on the pulpit, so he came down and prostrated. Then he recited it on the following Friday, and the people prepared themselves to prostrate, so he said: ""It was not prescribed for us except that we wish (to do it)."" So he did not prostrate, and they did not prostrate. Some of the people of knowledge have adopted this view, and it is the view of Ash-Shafi'i and Ahmad."
577,"Ibn Abbas narrated:""I saw the Messenger of Allah prostrating for (Surat) Sad."" Ibn Abbas said: ""It is not one of the resolute prostrations"
578,"Uqbah bin Amir narrated:""I said: 'O Messenger of Allah! Surah Al-Hajj has been esteemed by two prostrations?' He said: 'Yes"
579,"Al-Hasan bin Muhammad bin Ubaidullah bin Abi Yazid said:Ibn Juraij said to me: O Hasan! Ubaidullah bin Abi Yazid informed me that Ibn Abbas said: ""A man came to the Prophet and said: 'O Messenger of Allah! I had a dream at night while I was sleeping in which I was praying behind a tree"
580,"Aisha narrated:""When the Messenger of Allah would prostrate (for recitation of) the Qur'an"
581,"Umar bin Al-Khattab narrated that :the Messenger of Allah said: ""Whoever slept"
582,"Abu Hurairah narrated:""Muhammad said: 'Does not the one who raises his head before the Imam fear that Allah will transform his head into a donkey's head?"
583,"Jabir bin Abdullah narrated:'Mu'adh bin Jabal would pray Al-Maghrib with the Messenger of Allah"
584,"Anas bin Malik narrated:""When we prayed behind the Prophet in the midday heat"
585,"Jabir bin Samurah narrated:""When the Prophet prayed Fajr he would remain seated where he had prayed until the sun had risen"
586,"Anas bin Malik narrated that :the Messenger of Allah said: ""Whoever prays Fajr in congregation"
587,"Ibn Abbas narrated:""The Messenger of Allah would glance toward the right and the left during Salat but he would not turn his neck to look behind him"
588,"Sa'eed bin Abi Hind narrated from some of the companions of Ikrimah:""The Prophet would glance during Salat"" and he mentioned a similar narration"
589,"Anas narrated:""The Messenger of Allah said to me: 'O my son! Beware of looking around during the Salat"
590,"Aishah narrated:""I asked the Messenger of Allah about looking around during the Salat. He said: 'It is a portion which the Shaitan snatches during a man's prayer"
591,"Mu'adh bin Jabal narrated that :the Messenger of Allah said: ""When one of you comes to the Salat and (finds) the Imam is in a position"
592,"Abdullah bin Abi Qatadah narrated from his father that :the Messenger of Allah said: ""When the Iqamah is called for the prayer"
593,"# Hadith Translation

## Ḥadīth No. — On the Supplication During the Prayer (Tashahhud)

**Chain of Narration:**

Maḥmūd ibn Ghaylān narrated to us — Yaḥyā ibn Ādam narrated to us — Abū Bakr ibn ʿAyyāsh narrated to us — from ʿĀṣim — from Zirr — from ʿAbdullāh (may Allāh be pleased with him), who said:

---

**The Narration:**

I was praying, and the Prophet (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa sallam), along with Abū Bakr and ʿUmar (may Allāh be pleased with them both), were with him. When I sat [in the tashahhud], I began with extolling the praise of Allāh, then sending ṣalawāt upon the Prophet (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa sallam), then I supplicated for myself. Thereupon the Prophet (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa sallam) said:

> **""Ask, and you shall be given. Ask, and you shall be given.""**

*(He repeated it twice.)*

---

**Additional Narrations:**

He (Imam al-Tirmidhī) said: There is also a narration on this authority from Faḍālah ibn ʿUbayd (may Allāh be pleased with him).

**Imam Abū ʿĪsā al-Tirmidhī said:** The ḥadīth of ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd (may Allāh be pleased with him) is a **ḥasan ṣaḥīḥ** (good and authentic) ḥadīth.

**He also said:** This ḥadīth was also narrated by Aḥmad ibn Ḥanbal from Yaḥyā ibn Ādam in an abridged form.

---

*— Extracted from Jāmiʿ al-Tirmidhī (Sunan al-Tirmidhī), Book of Ṣalāh (Prayer)*"
594,"Aishah narrated:""The Prophet ordered the construction of Masajid in all Dur and that they be kept clean and scented"
595,"It was narrated to us by Hannad, it was narrated to us by Abdah and Waki', from Hisham ibn Urwah, from his father, that the Prophet (peace be upon him) commanded, and he mentioned something similar to it. Abu Isa said: This hadith is more authentic than the first hadith."
596,"Narrated to us was Ibn Abi 'Umar, narrated to us was Sufyan ibn 'Uyaynah, from Hisham ibn 'Urwah, from his father, that the Prophet (peace be upon him) commanded and mentioned something similar to it. Sufyan said: His statement, ""with the construction of masjids in the houses,"" refers to the tribes."
597,"Ibn Umar narrated that :the Prophet said: ""The Salat during the night and the day is two and two"
598,"Asim bin Damrah said:""We asked Ali about the prayer of the Messenger of Allah during the day. He said: 'You will not be able to do that.' We said: 'Whoever among is able (he will)?' So he said: 'When the sun appeared over there (east) like it appears here (west) at Asr"
599,"# Hadith Translation

**Narrated by 'Alī (may Allah be pleased with him), from the Prophet ﷺ, with a similar narration:**

Muḥammad ibn al-Muthannā narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn Ja'far narrated to us: Shu'bah reported to us, from Abū Isḥāq, from 'Āṣim ibn Ḍamrah, from 'Alī (may Allah be pleased with him), from the Prophet ﷺ, a narration of similar import.

Abū 'Īsā (al-Tirmidhī) said: *""This is a ḥasan (good) hadith.""*

Isḥāq ibn Ibrāhīm said: *""The finest narration that has been transmitted regarding the voluntary prayers of the Prophet ﷺ during the daytime is this one.""*

It has been reported that 'Abdullāh ibn al-Mubārak used to consider this hadith as weak (ḍa'īf).

**Reason for its weakness:** According to us — and Allah knows best — it was deemed weak because a narration of this nature is not reported from the Prophet ﷺ except through this particular chain, namely from 'Āṣim ibn Ḍamrah, from 'Alī (may Allah be pleased with him).

'Āṣim ibn Ḍamrah is considered trustworthy (thiqah) by some of the scholars of hadith.

'Alī ibn al-Madīnī said: Yaḥyā ibn Sa'īd al-Qaṭṭān reported that Sufyān (al-Thawrī) said: *""We used to recognize the superiority of the hadith of 'Āṣim ibn Ḍamrah over the hadith of al-Ḥārith.""*

---

*Note: This narration appears in Sunan al-Tirmidhī, where the author discusses the scholarly evaluation of its chain of transmission, noting both its acceptance by some authorities and its criticism by others due to its singular route of transmission.*"
600,"Aishah narrated:""The Messenger of Allah would not pray in women's covers"
601,"Urwah narrated that Aishah said:""I came while the Messenger of Allah was praying in the house and the door was closed. So he walked until he opened the door for me"
602,"Abu Wa'il said:""A man asked Abdullah bin Mas'ud about this phrase: 'Ghairi asin' or is it Yasin? So he said: 'You can recite all of the Quran besides this [phrase]?' He said: 'Yes.' He said: 'Indeed a people recite it"
603,"Abu Hurairah narrated that :the Prophet said: ""When a man performs Wudu and he performs his Wudu well"
604,"Sa'd bin Ishaq bin Ka'b bin Ujrah narrated from his father from his grandfather who said:""The Prophet prayed Maghrib in the Masjid of Banu Abdul-Ashbal"
605,"Qais bin Asim narrated that:he accepted Islam and the Prophet ordered him to perform Ghusl with water and Sidr"
606,"Ali bin Abi Talid (may Allah be pleased with him) narrated that :the Messenger of Allah said: ""The screen between the eyes of the jinns and nakedness of the children of Adam when one of you enters the area of relieving oneself is saying: 'Bismillah"
607,"Abdullah bin Busr narrated that :the Prophet said: ""On the day of Resurrection"
608,"Aishah narrated:""The Messenger of Allah would love to start with the right side when he purified himself"
609,"Anas bin Malik narrated that :the Messenger of Allah said: The acceptable Wudu is with two Ratils of water"
610,"Muhammad bin Bashshar narrated to us, Mu'adh bin Hisham narrated to us, my father narrated to me, from Qatadah, from Abu Harb bin Abi Al-Aswad, from his father, from 'Ali bin Abi Talib, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said regarding the urine of a nursing male infant: ""The urine of a boy is to be sprinkled with water, while the urine of a girl is to be washed."" Qatadah said: ""This is when they have not yet eaten food; but if they have eaten, then both should be washed.""

Abu 'Eisa said: This is a Hasan hadith. Hisham Ad-Dastawa'i reported this hadith from Qatadah as a raised (marfu') hadith, while Sa'eed bin Abi 'Arubah reported it from Qatadah as a stopped (mawquf) hadith, without raising it."
611,"Shahr bin Hawshab said:""I saw Jarir bin Abdullah performing Wudu and wiping over his Khuff."" He said: ""So I asked him: 'What is that?' He said: 'I saw the Prophet performing Wudu and he wiped over his Khuff.' So I said to him: 'Before Al-Ma'idah or after Al-Ma'idah?' He said: 'I did not accept Islam until after Al-Ma'idah"
612,"Shahr bin Hawshab said:(Another chain) from Khalid bin Ziyad with similar"
613,"Ammar narrated:""The Prophet permitted the Junub when he wanted to eat"
614,"Ka'b bin Ujrah narrated:""The Messenger of Allah said to me: 'I seek refuge in Allah for you O Ka'b bin Ujrah from leader that will be after me. Whoever comes to their doors to approve of their lies and supports them in their oppression"
615,"Muhammad said:""Ibn Numair narrated to us from Ubaidullah bin Musa"
616,"Sulaim bin Amir narrated:""I heard Abu Umamah saying: I heard the Messenger of Allah giving a Khutbah during the Farewell Hajj"
